            <div xml:id="d592">

                <pb facs="0443" n="439" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg439"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">394.1123 Thomson, J. C./41: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Japan</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-08-13T14:00:00-04:00">August 13, 1938—2
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>287. Your 527, August 11, 5 p.m., Thomson case.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>After careful consideration of all the circumstances attending the Thomson
                        case and the resulting discussions with the Japanese authorities, the
                        Department, being convinced that no fault lay with the American side, does
                        not feel that it can properly consent to any statement which would further
                        modify in favor of the Japanese side the statement proposed in paragraph 2
                        of the Department’s 267.<anchor n="53a" xml:id="d592-f1"/></item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Bearing in mind, however, the last sentences of paragraphs 1 and 6 of your
                        500, July 29, 11 p.m., the Department is inclined, in the absence of further
                        unfavorable developments, to take no initiative in regard to publicity in
                        this long drawn out and still unsettled case.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>You may in your discretion, and subject to any further comment that you
                        may wish to make to the Department, inform the Japanese Foreign Office in
                        the above sense.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
